A||N||6||Daniel87GT||I want my oil pan off!||||1||
Z||000000||Daniel87GT||07-27-2001||03:08 PM||||I'm trying desperately to change the oil pan gasket on my 87 GT.  I pulled the starter, but am having a tough time getting around the engine mount.  It looks like it could do it if only there weren't those long bolts holding it on instead of the screws that are everywhere else.  I'm tempted to just cut the one off.  The bolt does have a torx type top to it.  Does it come out?  Any thoughts?<P>I've searched the archives; people have suggested that you "just go get a bottle jack and jack that side of the engine up."  I can't find a good spot to put the jack.  I wish I owned an engine hoist.  Can anyone help me out on where to put the jack?||||reg||1
Z||000001||GT GT 87||07-27-2001||03:29 PM||||I had the same problem on my "87.  I didn't want to un do the engine mount from the engine , they are hard to line up again.  I am a carpenter , so I took my trusty sawsall with a metal cutting blade and notched the mount to clear the pan. You have to experiment turning the crank till the crank is up out of the way . This is a little hard but putting it back on is a snap   ............||||reg||1
Z||000002||87GTZ34||07-27-2001||04:44 PM||||I just had my pan off and aligning the mount was not a problem.  I perform all of my own repairs by my self so I use jacks and jack stands.  It really was no trouble to change the pan gasket that way by myself so go for it.  GL||||reg||1
Z||000003||fsrowsell||07-27-2001||11:55 PM||||I just changed my oil pan gasket last week by myself. This is what I did: I jacked the car up on jack stands. Lowered my exhause, because it was in the way as will. I took one of the starter bolts out and loosen the other one so that I could move the starter enough to get at two of the pan bolts. I then removed the right wheel and the splash guards so I could get access to the two motor mount bolts on the engine block. I placed my jack under my oil pan with a peace of 2x4 and took the pressure off the motor mount bolts. Then I removed the two motor mount bolts. Then jacked the engine up enough to place two peaces of 2x4 under the belt pulley. Then I rested the engine on the two peaces of 2x4. I then unbolted the pan and dropped it with no problems and I had no problems lining up the motor mount bolts again. It was a little time consuming but it worked great. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||PBJ||07-28-2001||12:56 AM||||as mentioned above, just cut a small section out of the mtr mount.  usually I would just "torch out" a small piece. saw will work. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Will||07-28-2001||04:52 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fsrowsell:<BR><B>I just changed my oil pan gasket last week by myself. This is what I did: I jacked the car up on jack stands. Lowered my exhause, because it was in the way as will. I took one of the starter bolts out and loosen the other one so that I could move the starter enough to get at two of the pan bolts. I then removed the right wheel and the splash guards so I could get access to the two motor mount bolts on the engine block. I placed my jack under my oil pan with a peace of 2x4 and took the pressure off the motor mount bolts. Then I removed the two motor mount bolts. Then jacked the engine up enough to place two peaces of 2x4 under the belt pulley. Then I rested the engine on the two peaces of 2x4. I then unbolted the pan and dropped it with no problems and I had no problems lining up the motor mount bolts again. It was a little time consuming but it worked great. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That is the proper way to do it. You don't have to cut anything. Just jack the engine up and support it with 2x4 blocks under the crank pulley while removing the two bolts which hold the mount bracket to the engine block.<P>I changed the oil pump in a friend's 87GT this way.||||reg||1
Z||000006||84 2in1||07-28-2001||06:55 PM||||I dont know about the V-6 but 0n a 88 I-4 i just took a 2x4 an ran it across the engine compartment and wraped a chain around the wood and ran it thru the hoist loop then took the bolts loose from the mount, raised the board at the pully end of the motor and put a 4x4 blockunder it to hold everything up while the mount and bracket were out. -B-||||reg||1
